## Roles & permissions
Two kinds of permission, with a hard boundary between them:

- **Account administration** (`user`, `session`: list, create, ban, set-role, impersonate...) belongs to the Better Auth `admin` plugin. It is compiled in `packages/auth/src/permissions.ts` (`accountStatement`, from the plugin's `defaultStatements`) and only the `admin` role holds it. It is never stored and no role created at runtime can gain it. `admin` is `adminRoles` in `auth.ts`; the plugin checks it itself.
- **Business resources** are the vocabulary of `businessStatement` in the same file: the only resources and actions a role can be given, and the only source of valid names. What each role may do on them is **data**, read from the database on every request:
  - `roles` has one row per role: `id` is the value of `users.role`, plus a `label`.
  - `role_permissions` has one row per permission: `role_id`, `resource`, `action`, unique on the three, deleted with the role.
{{> (blueprintPartial "docs-business")}}

`admin` has no rows. `permissionsOf` (`packages/auth/src/role-permissions.ts`) gives it everything it is compiled with, every business action included, without reading the database. Any other role holds exactly the rows it has: a role missing from `roles`, a role with no rows, an empty database all hold nothing, so a partial setup refuses. A row is honored only when it names `businessStatement`, so a `user.delete` row written by hand grants nothing.

`permissionsOf` reads what a role holds and `isGranted` tests a permission against that list; `hasPermission(db, role, resource, action)` is the two together, for a caller outside tRPC. They keep nothing between calls, so a permission removed from `role_permissions` is refused on the next request. Inside tRPC `callerPermissions` (`packages/api/src/middleware/rbac.ts`) reads the list once for a whole request (every procedure of a batch or of a page's prefetches shares it, keyed by the request's `Headers` in a `WeakMap`, so it dies with the request), and `permissionProcedure` hands it to the procedures below it as `ctx.permissions`.

The compiled `roles` of `permissions.ts` exist because the `admin` plugin rejects a role name it does not know when it assigns one. Only `admin` holds permissions there; `user` and `manager` are accepted names with none. A role you create in the database does not need to be compiled: it is assigned through `{{router}}.setRole`, which `guardAdministrators` carries out itself. Every Better Auth upgrade should re-check this boundary: the plugin still has no dynamic access control, which is why the roles live in application tables.

### Default roles
No migration is shipped, so the tables start empty. {{#if (hasRootScript "db:roles:remote")}}`{{runScript "db:roles"}}` (local) and `{{runScript "db:roles:remote"}}` (production) write{{else}}`{{runScript "db:roles"}}` writes{{/if}} the roles of `DEFAULT_ROLES` in `packages/auth/src/default-roles.ts`:

{{> (blueprintPartial "docs-default-roles")}}

`{{runScript "local-setup"}}` runs {{#if (hasRootScript "db:roles:remote")}}the local one, and the deploy steps run the remote one after the migrations{{else}}it{{/if}}. The defaults are written once, into a database that has no role: once any role exists the command writes nothing, so a default role you deleted, a permission you revoked and an emptied matrix are never written back, and running it again changes nothing. {{#if (has "database" "d1")}}If a run was interrupted half way (roles with no permission), delete the rows of `roles` and run it again.{{else}}The roles and their permissions are written in one transaction, so a run that stops half way writes nothing, and two runs at once write the roles once.{{/if}} Every definition is validated against `businessStatement` before anything is written (`defaultRoleRows` in `default-roles.ts`), so a default can never name `user` or `session` or the reserved `admin`.
